-- Regression test for a bug found with Alan 3.0beta8 (filed 2026-08-12).
--
-- An aggregate (SUM/MAX/MIN OF <attr> IN <container>) used to be rejected when
-- the container's TAKING class was ITSELF a container declared with a bare
-- CONTAINER (no Taking -> defaults to 'thing'). Aggregating transitively means
-- that anything in the nested container might turn up too, so the class that
-- can be guaranteed for all of them is the JOIN of the taking classes, which
-- collapses to 'thing', and an attribute defined on the real taking class was
-- rejected with:
--
--   316 E : Attribute 'cover' is not defined for instances aggregated over
--           using 'SUM' since the class it is guaranteed to be in this
--           context ('thing') does not have it.
--
-- The generated code only aggregates over instances of the class defining the
-- attribute, so a single class among the possible contents defining it is
-- enough. Below, 'closet' TAKES garment and 'cover' is defined on garment, so
-- 'SUM OF cover IN closet' is valid and must compile. See
-- aggregate_taking_container_ambiguous for the case that must still be an error.
--
-- Found while play-testing the Wyldkynd game: EVERY clothing had a bare CONTAINER
-- ("to allow a wallet in a jacket"), which broke the library's
-- SUM OF topcover IN worn (worn CONTAINER TAKING clothing).

The room Isa location
End the.

The hero Isa actor At room
  Is total 0.
End the.

The closet Isa entity
  Container Taking garment.
End the.

Every garment Isa thing
  Is cover 0.
  Is washed.
  Container            -- bare container (defaults to Taking thing): the trigger
End every.

Verb probe
  Does
    Set total Of hero To Sum Of cover In closet.
    -- The attribute filters may use attributes of the same class, since the
    -- generated existance filter has already skipped everything that is not
    -- a garment before any filter is applied.
    Set total Of hero To Sum Of cover Is washed, In closet.
End verb.

Start At room.
